h4. API

The site answers the same routes as JSON or XML when asked: add ".json" or ".xml" to a URL, or send an Accept header. Searches take the same parameters as the pages do.

h4. Authentication

Generate an API key from your account page. Send it with your user name using HTTP Basic auth, or as login and api_key parameters. Each key can be limited to specific permissions and IP addresses.

h4. What is different here, and it matters

[b]Anonymous requests from outside a browser do not reach the site.[/b] Every signed-out request is given a Cloudflare challenge that only a browser can solve, and a request that fails it receives a 403 from the edge -- with a cf-mitigated header, and without the x-request-id header the application would send. curl, scripts and monitoring all get this by design.

A script that needs the API must therefore carry a session: sign in with a browser, take the _danbooru2_session cookie, and send it with each request. An API key alone is not a session and will be challenged.

Comments, notes and the forum are not part of this site; their API routes return 404.
